# Copy this to `.env` and fill it in. `.env` itself is gitignored, so a fresh
# clone and every new worktree starts without one — which is why this file
# exists and why it explains itself rather than just listing keys.
#
# AGENTS.md has pointed readers here for some time. Until now the file was not
# in the repository at all: `.env*` in .gitignore swallowed it, so nobody could
# commit one. That is the whole reason three sessions lost time to the entry
# below after it had twice been "already fixed" — the fix was real and the
# place it was written down did not exist.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The local development database from docker-compose.local.yml, managed with
# `pnpm local:db:up`. Port 55434 deliberately: not 5432, and not 55432 or
# 55433, which other local projects on this machine already hold.
#
# Every worktree on a machine shares this one database. That is worth knowing
# before you run anything: two Playwright runs against it collide however
# different their WEB_PORTs are, because the port is not the lock — the
# database is.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://gomoku:gomoku@localhost:55434/gomoku"

# Direct (unpooled) connection, used for migrations. Locally the same as
# DATABASE_URL; in production it is the unpooled Neon endpoint.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://gomoku:gomoku@localhost:55434/gomoku"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Any random string locally. `openssl rand -base64 32` will do.
AUTH_SECRET="replace-me"

# Comma-separated. These addresses reach the Admin page and the operator views.
ADMIN_EMAILS="you@example.com"

# Lets the e2e suite and local tooling act as the operator without Google.
ADMIN_TOKEN="replace-me"

# Lets an agent's terminal write to the features board through the API,
# with an actor name, and nothing else. Distinct from ADMIN_TOKEN so it can
# be rotated on its own and can never open a browser session.
# BOARD_TOKEN="replace_me"

# Google sign-in for the operator. Both values come from the Google console
# OAuth client. Local development registers http://localhost:6600 as a redirect
# URI; leave these empty if you are not exercising Google sign-in.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=""
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=""

# The dev server's own address locally. In production this is the real domain
# and belongs in Vercel rather than in this file.
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:6600"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RATE_LIMIT_RELIEF — read this one before running the e2e suite
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# **Set this, or roughly a dozen e2e tests fail with 429s and nothing says why.**
#
# The suite drives the whole site from one address and creates a game in most
# of its three hundred tests, which trips limits meant for one household. The
# failures surface in files testing something else entirely, so they read as
# real bugs in whatever spec happened to be in flight. Three sessions have lost
# time to this, twice after it had already been solved elsewhere.
#
# It multiplies only the limits that exist to bound a cost. It never touches
# the guessing paths — the invite-code and token limits stay exactly as strict,
# because those bound an attack rather than an expense — and it is ignored
# outright in production. Both of those are held by tests in
# src/lib/api/rateLimit.ts's suite, so neither can quietly stop being true.
#
# THE TRAP, which is not obvious and has no warning anywhere else:
#
# This one variable ALSO multiplies the twenty-game cap. See activeGameLimit()
# in src/lib/history/activeGames.ts — with the relief at 20, a member may hold
# 400 active games rather than 20. That is deliberate (one knob rather than two
# was judged cheaper than a second thing to forget), and it has a consequence
# worth stating plainly:
#
#   **Any browser test of the twenty-game cap is a guaranteed false pass.**
#
# It will not hit a cap of 400, so it proves nothing and reports green. At the
# time of writing no e2e spec asserts the cap, so nothing is currently lying —
# but the cap's trigger is therefore untested end to end, and cannot be tested
# while one variable governs both. If you are about to write that test, split
# the knob first.
#
# Leave it unset outside the suite. A relief nobody set changes nothing.
RATE_LIMIT_RELIEF=20

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The dev server's port. Defaults to 6600. Give a worktree its own, or
# Playwright's `reuseExistingServer` will adopt whatever is already listening
# and run your specs against another worktree's code — a failure that looks
# exactly like your own feature being broken.
# WEB_PORT=6600

# SITE_MAINTENANCE — the shutter, and the only site-wide control that is not a
# switch on /admin.
#
# Set to `on` and every request but the operator's is refused at the gate with a
# 503 and a short notice, before a line of the app runs. Unset, or set to
# anything else at all, and it does nothing — see `maintenanceIsOn`, which is
# named rather than negated on purpose: a site shut by a value nobody meant as a
# shutter is unreachable for a reason nobody outside the code can see.
#
# It is a variable rather than a row in SiteSetting because `src/proxy.ts` reads
# it on EVERY request. A gate that asked Postgres each time would be a cost
# fault and latency on every page — and, worse, it could not answer during the
# hour the database is being worked on, which is the hour this exists for. It
# would fail closed against the one person who has to get in and reopen the
# site. WazaDB took its own maintenance check back OUT of middleware for the
# first of those reasons; its surviving gate-level switch is an environment
# variable read exactly like this one.
#
# Getting back out, in the order to try it:
#   1. The operator's session passes the shutter, so /admin still works.
#   2. /join, /api/session and /api/auth stay open, so the operator can sign in
#      from a browser holding no cookie at all.
#   3. Failing both, remove this variable and redeploy. That needs no database
#      write and no request the gate will ever see.
#
# To stop NEW MEMBERS without shutting the site — much the commoner want — use
# "Nobody new" under Admin → The site, which is a row, takes effect at once, and
# leaves everybody already signed up exactly as they were.
# SITE_MAINTENANCE=on
